n\n**Scans the query forces**\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email) = '...'` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database has to read every user row and lower-case each email.\n2. **Full scan of `orders`, unless `orders.user_id` is already indexed.** The join key `o.user_id` needs an index so the lookup is a few index probes. Without one, the planner scans or hashes the whole `orders` table.\n3. **Wide rows from `SELECT *`.** This isn't a scan, but it pulls every column from both tables. That rules out index-only scans and returns duplicate column names such as `id`, which can break clients that map columns by name.\n\n**Fixes**\n\nPick one of the first two options for `users`. Both assume Postgres, so adjust the syntax for other engines.\n\n```sql\n-- Option A: expression index, keeps the query as written\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- Option B: normalize on write (or use citext), then query the plain column\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_idx ON users (email);\n--   ... WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'\n\n-- orders side\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nOption B is cleaner long-term, because the application guarantees emails are stored lower-case. Option A is the smaller change if you can't touch the data.\n\n**Rewrite**\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total   -- only the columns you need\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nI haven't seen your schema, so I assumed the indexes above don't exist yet and picked the order columns as examples. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after to confirm the plan moves from `Seq Scan` to `Index Scan` on both tables.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}